 # This class is part of an EXPERIMENTAL API.
class CorrectionService:
    """CorrectionService changes archived data on purpose, and keeps the record
    of each change: delete a subject, a session, a scan or an instance;
    relabel a subject, a session or a scan; replace an archived instance with
    the corrected file that was quarantined as a different file under its
    UID. It also lists what a scrub found (ListScrubFindings), the other way
    archived data comes to differ from what was archived.

    Every correction has a reason a person gives, 1 to 1024 bytes of UTF-8
    with no control characters, in their own words, such as "consent
    withdrawn" or "wrong subject id at the scanner". The reason is kept on
    the correction, in its audit entry and in its correction record on the
    storage (which holds patient data already), and is shown to whoever
    reads the correction; it must never hold a value read from a file (a
    name, a birth date, a UID), since a log and the correction keep it for
    as long as they are kept.

    One state machine, two authorities. Every correction moves through
    CorrectionState: prepared, committed, purging, purged; or undone (a
    delete, from committed) or abandoned (from prepared, never committed).
    Its row in the tenant's database is committed first, prepared, with its
    operation id (Correction.id), target, reason and audit entry, before
    anything is written to a storage. Then a correction record,
    "meta/.corrections/<operation id>.json", is put on each storage the
    target touches, naming the operation, its kind and state, its reason,
    the key that made it, the senders its data was sent to, and the target's
    scans (labels never start with a dot, so this is never a subject's
    tree). Then one publishing transaction makes the change visible, the row
    committed, with an EVENT_TYPE_CATALOG_CHANGED event; then each record is
    rewritten with the new state, as at every later step. The database is
    the authority while it lives; the records are what a rebuild or a
    restore reads without it.

    When a correction stops short. A request that fails before its
    publishing transaction abandons its correction at once: anything it
    wrote is removed, the answer is the error, and the correction stays
    listed as abandoned with its reason. At start, a delete or a replace
    left prepared by a stop is abandoned likewise (its request was never
    answered: it did not happen), and a relabel left prepared goes on with
    its copy from where it was, since it runs in the background by design.
    A prepared relabel is stopped by UndoCorrection, which abandons it and
    removes its copies. A rebuild or a restore that finds a record with no
    row applies it fail-closed, a prepared delete as committed, since showing
    withdrawn data is the worse error, and makes it a row marked unresolved,
    with the record's reason, key and senders, the state_reason "found on
    storage without its row", and no purge_time: it is never purged until an
    admin confirms it (PurgeCorrection) or undoes it (UndoCorrection).
    Records, like deleted_targets, are kept for as long as the tenant is: a
    restore from a backup older than a delete applies them before the tenant
    serves.

    A delete. It first hides its data and only later removes its bytes, so a
    mistyped label can be undone. (1) Preview: a Delete with validate_only
    answers what would go, counts and bytes, the storages that hold it and
    what each will do with a removed object (DeletePreview.storage_purges),
    the senders it was sent to and the jobs that would meet it, and a
    confirm token: a digest of the target, the counts and bytes, the
    storages, the senders, the jobs, the purge expectations, the holds and
    what retention keeps (holds, sessions_under_minimum, keep_until) and the
    time it was issued (not purge_time, which moves with the clock). (2) Act: a
    Delete with the same target and that token, within an hour of its issue.
    When the answer would differ (data archived under the target since the
    preview, a job made), the token no longer matches and the delete is
    refused, so a delete acts on what a person saw. A token is used once: a
    Delete that carries a token already used, with the same target, answers
    the correction that token made, whatever its state, so a client that
    lost the answer can send the same request again; with another target it
    is refused (precondition). The server remembers a used token while it
    runs, for at most about the last 1024 deletes: after a restart, or once
    forgotten, the repeat is answered not found, since its target is hidden.
    Its
    publishing transaction hides the catalog's rows from every read (the
    catalog, DICOMweb, reads and downloads answer as if they did not exist)
    and moves the totals. Queued and running jobs that target the data meet
    it as deleted (JOB_FAILURE_CODE_DELETED_IN_ARCHIVE) and go on. An
    instance delete hides that instance alone; the scan and its other
    instances stay as they are, and at purge a new manifest without it is
    published in one transaction, so the others never leave the catalog. A
    scan whose last instance is deleted is deleted as a scan.

    The grace period and the purge. The bytes stay on the storage, unserved,
    for the tenant's setting delete.grace (a period such as "7d", from "0d"
    to "90d"; built-in default "7d"), measured from commit_time by the
    server's clock and fixed then as purge_time: a later change of the
    setting moves no purge already set, and no DICOM date is read for it.
    UndoCorrection during that time shows a delete's data again as it was.
    Then the server purges: each object is read for its size and SHA-256,
    compared with the manifest's, and removed through its storage, which
    says what became of it (ObjectDisposition): removed, hidden behind a
    newer version, retained by the storage's object lock, or not known.
    Each object is recorded with its size, checksum and disposition; then
    the manifest and metadata; then the catalog's rows, the correction
    purged. The correction says, per storage, what the storage did
    (Correction.dispositions), and never claims more than it reported (see
    "What purged means" below). A purge that stops midway (a crash,
    a storage that does not answer) goes on from the row and the records.
    PurgeCorrection purges at once, before the grace ends. Once a purge has
    started, nothing is undone. A purge that starts because its purge_time
    came, rather than by PurgeCorrection, waits with the state reason
    "clock jumped" while the tenant's retention is paused for a clock jump
    (RetentionService): a clock that leapt forward must not make a grace
    period end early. The server compares its clock with the last time it
    trusted before it starts any purge by purge_time, and at least every
    hour, and pauses as the daily pass does when the clock has jumped.

    Arriving again. What names the deleted data is kept after the purge: a
    subject's label, a session's Study Instance UID, a scan's Series
    Instance UID, an instance's SOP Instance UID. A file that matches one
    later is quarantined as QUARANTINE_REASON_WAS_DELETED rather than
    archived, until an admin allows that target to arrive again
    (AllowArrivals), one value at a time, once the delete is purged. The
    preview says which subject labels stay held (held_after_purge); the
    correction lists every value it holds (Correction.holds). Undone and
    abandoned deletes keep nothing.

    What the archive cannot reach. Copies already sent to a peer by a send
    job or a receiver's forward are the peer's; the preview and the record
    list each sender and how many instances it was sent, so a person can ask
    the peer to delete them. A file a client downloaded before is the
    client's: an instance deleted answers not found, and a replaced instance
    has a new SHA-256 and so a new ETag, so a download resumed with If-Range
    (RFC 9110 13.1.5) gets the new file whole, never the two joined. Events
    already in the feed keep the labels they carried until events.retention.

    A key is written once. Relabel and replace never overwrite a stored
    object: new bytes, or the same bytes under new labels, go to new keys,
    the publishing transaction switches readers to them, and the old objects
    stay, unserved, until the correction's purge after delete.grace. So a
    read that began before the publish finishes on the old object, whole
    and matching the checksum it was given, and no reader ever gets one
    revision's checksum with another's bytes.

    Relabel. The labels are part of every stored object's key, so a relabel
    copies each object of the target to its key under the new label, reads
    the copy back and compares its SHA-256, and writes new manifests and
    metadata; this is the long part, and it runs in the background and goes
    on after a restart, readers seeing only the old labels throughout. Then
    one publishing transaction switches the whole target: no reader ever
    sees part of a subject under one label and part under the other. The
    old tree is purged after the grace period. A lookup by an old label
    answers not found with the reason RELABELED, naming the new label. An
    object of the target that cannot be read whole stops the copy: the
    relabel is abandoned, its copies removed, and nothing changed for any
    reader.

    Replace. Only from a held quarantined file whose SOP Instance UID the
    tenant holds in the same study and series: one of reason
    QUARANTINE_REASON_DIFFERENT_FILE (the corrected file a sender sent
    again), or of reason QUARANTINE_REASON_REPAIRS_DAMAGED (the same file
    sent again for an instance a scrub marked damaged). The file is first
    de-identified by the profile version of the receiver it arrived at, as a
    release would (QuarantineService), and is never archived as received
    when that receiver de-identifies. Its new bytes are put at the
    instance's next revision key, never at the key it has, read back and
    compared; a new manifest and metadata naming the new revision are
    written; then the publishing transaction gives the instance its new
    revision and checksum, keeps the old revision's checksum as superseded
    (ResolveInstances answers it), and clears the instance's damage mark, so
    it reads again. A replace is not undone: the way back is another replace
    from the old file. A Replace of a quarantined file already used by a
    replace answers that correction, so a lost answer is safe to ask again.

    Every copy. A correction reaches every storage that holds its target: the
    primary and each of its replicas (StorageService). It puts its record on
    the primary before its publishing transaction, itself, never left to the
    copy, and on each replica too; a replica that does not answer is owed
    the record (ReplicaService), written in the publishing transaction, and
    gets it as soon as it answers, so a replica down never stops a
    correction. A delete's publish also drops what the copy still owes the
    replicas of its target's scans. Its purge takes the set of copies when
    it starts: the primary and each replica attached then, so a replica
    attached after the commit, which holds the record and was never sent
    the hidden data, is checked too; a replica attached while the purge
    runs joins the set, and the copy never takes an object of a correction
    whose purge has started. It removes each object from every copy and
    records what each storage did, one disposition per storage and
    switch_generation (each naming the storage, whether it is a replica,
    and the place it acted at), so a purge that spans a switch says what
    each place did. A replica that does not answer, or that is still
    owed the record, keeps the correction purging, with the state reason
    "storage does not answer", never skipped; only DetachReplica with
    abandon_purges ends that wait, and the replica is then named in
    copies_abandoned. A replace's publish queues the scan for each replica,
    and its old revision is purged from every copy after the grace period.
    A relabel copies to the new keys on the primary and on each replica in
    step for the target that answers, and rewrites the hold records of what
    it moves (RetentionService), before its publishing transaction, so a
    read can turn to a replica the moment the labels switch; a replica not
    in step or not answering is not waited for: it is copied afterwards,
    under the new labels, as any pending scan. The preview lists every copy
    (storage_purges). A withdrawal is not true while any copy the server
    wrote still holds the bytes.

    What purged means. Purged is the end of a purge, every object of every
    copy handled and its disposition recorded; it never claims more than the
    storages reported. The bytes are gone from a storage only where its
    disposition counts every object removed; a copy whose objects are
    hidden behind newer versions, retained by object lock, unknown, or
    abandoned with a detached replica (copies_abandoned) still holds them,
    and the correction says so per storage. So a person reads, for each
    copy, whether a withdrawal is complete there.

    Retention and holds. A delete may come from a person (origin person) or
    from a retention rule (origin retention, RetentionService): the latter
    is made by a dispose job (DisposeJob), which the server's daily pass or
    an admin's Dispose queues from a rule's due sessions, and is then an
    ordinary delete in every way above
    (records, publish, grace, undo, purge on every copy, dispositions,
    arriving again). A hold (RetentionService) on what a delete would cover
    refuses the delete (precondition, reason HELD); a hold placed on data a
    delete already committed keeps the delete committed, still undone by
    UndoCorrection, and its purge waits, purging, with the state reason
    "held" before its next object, until every hold on it is released. A
    replace or a relabel under a hold is allowed, since nothing is lost, and
    the purge of the objects it superseded waits likewise. Data a rule keeps
    at least until a time to come (keep_at_least) is refused to a person's
    delete (precondition, reason RETENTION_MINIMUM) unless the request sets
    override_retention with its own reason, which the correction and its
    audit entry keep: a consent withdrawn must stay possible, and visible.

    References. The identities that never change are the archived UIDs; a
    label is a name and can change, and an instance's revision with its
    SHA-256 names exact bytes. ResolveInstances answers, for (instance,
    revision) pairs a platform kept, whether each is current, superseded,
    deleted or unknown.

    Every method acts inside one tenant, named by the request's tenant field,
    so a request into a tenant that is not ready is answered UNAVAILABLE.
    """

